---
title: "Translation"
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

NAHPU aims to be accessible to everyone, regardless of their native language. 
Translation lets them learn and use NAHPU in their preferred
language. We welcome translators at every level, from a single corrected word
to a complete new language.

NAHPU is currently translated into Brazilian Portuguese, Spanish, and
Indonesian. Translations are drafted with AI assistance and reviewed by people
who speak the language.

## Start simple

You do not need GitHub or any coding experience to help. The easiest way to
start is to leave a comment in the
[NAHPU translation spreadsheet](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1zG3vkcIgsfPMAal2lai876juXS3U_B4DHPEAxi1-Qko/edit?usp=sharing).
Point out a wrong or awkward translation, suggest a better term, or ask about
a word you are unsure of. A NAHPU team member will review your comment and
carry the change into the documentation or the app.

{% aside type="tip" %}
Comments from field biologists, curators, and collection managers are
especially valuable. You know which terms your colleagues actually use.
{% /aside %}

## Choose what to translate

| Area | What it covers | Guide |
| ---- | -------------- | ----- |
| Documentation | Usage guides, how-to recipes, and best practices on this website | [Documentation translation](./documentation) |
| App | Labels, buttons, messages, and settings inside the NAHPU app | [App translation](./app) |

Both areas share the same [Translation glossary](./glossary), so a concept
has the same name in the app and in the documentation. Read it before you
translate, and propose new entries when you meet a term it does not cover.

## Add a new language

We are always looking for speakers of languages NAHPU does not support yet.
A new language needs both the documentation and the app, so please reach out
to a [NAHPU team member](/teams) before you start. We will help you set up the
language and pair you with a reviewer.
